      "id": 1,
      "text": "Named after Leonidas Lucilius Steele, early settler who ran a hotel and mercantile in 1885.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steele%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Steele’s early growth hinged on being the only crossing over Little River Swamp to reach Cottonwood Point and the Mississippi River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steele%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1912, Steele boasted seven general stores, three cotton gins, a sawmill and a gristmill, reflecting its agricultural boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steele%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II, an auxiliary field northwest of Steele served as a B‑25 pilot training site for Blytheville Army Airfield.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steele%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s public library, part of the Missouri Library System, keeps the community connected to knowledge and history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steele%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Steele sits just five miles north of the Missouri‑Arkansas line, placing it in the heart of the Missouri Bootheel.",
